---
const responsibilities = [
  ['Programme Control', 'Holds priority, sequencing, dependencies and governed programme state.'],
  ['Company Control', 'Supports daily working context without independently owning programme truth.'],
  ['Platform Core', 'Provides shared identities, contracts, schemas and boundaries.'],
  ['NEOS', 'Observes engineering and implementation reality, including drift.'],
  ['GAIA', 'Supports bounded reasoning, analysis and recommendation.'],
  ['Command Centre', 'Provides human visibility, review, approval and governed orchestration.'],
  ['Knowledge / Librarian', 'Organises knowledge, evidence and governed intake.'],
  ['Foundries', 'Support bounded creation and production lifecycles.'],
  ['Infrastructure / Runtime', 'Provides the operating environment, with observability and recoverability as requirements.'],
  ['Governed Tools', 'Carry out permitted actions within approved scope, with verification and evidence.'],
];

const acceptanceQuestions = [
  ['What exists?', 'Which systems, capabilities and important records are actually present?'],
  ['Which source should we trust?', 'What is authoritative, and what is historical, experimental or still uncertain?'],
  ['What is active?', 'What is genuinely underway rather than merely selected or prepared?'],
  ['What comes next?', 'What has been prioritised through the proper decision process?'],
  ['What is implemented?', 'What exists in working form rather than only in a plan or interface?'],
  ['What is healthy — and what is drifting?', 'Where does current evidence differ from expectations?'],
  ['What depends on what?', 'What could be affected by a change?'],
  ['Who has authority?', 'Who owns the decision, and who must approve it?'],
  ['What may act?', 'Which tools or people are permitted to perform the specific action?'],
  ['What actually happened?', 'What changed, rather than what was intended?'],
  ['Was it verified?', 'What checks support the result?'],
  ['Where is the evidence?', 'Can the decision and its outcome be traced?'],
  ['Can we recover?', 'Is there a verified route back if something goes wrong?'],
];

const cycle = [
  'Direction and priorities',
  'Trusted context and observation',
  'Recommendation and impact',
  'Human decision',
  'Authorised action',
  'Verification and evidence',
  'Learning and reconciliation',
];
---

<article class="operating-baseline shell">
  <header class="operating-baseline__hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">New Earth Operating Baseline</p>
    <h1>Building the company that can understand itself</h1>
    <p class="lede">New Earth is building the ability to understand, operate, verify and evolve itself without losing track of truth, responsibility, authority or evidence.</p>
    <aside class="operating-baseline__status" aria-label="Current Operating Baseline status">
      <p>OPERATING BASELINE V1.0</p>
      <strong>In development</strong>
      <span>Initial architecture, identity and programme-control foundations have been accepted. Daily-work operating-layer reconciliation is underway. Full integration, end-to-end proof and formal V1.0 acceptance remain ahead.</span>
    </aside>
    <p>“Understand itself” means people being able to understand the organisation through reliable records, evidence and governed tools. It does not mean a conscious or sentient company, or AI governing New Earth autonomously.</p>
  </header>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-why">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why this foundation matters</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-why">Keeping an expanding organisation understandable</h2>
    <p>As New Earth grows, its tools, products, projects, documents, people and historical versions need more than individual progress. They need a reliable way to preserve what is true, who is responsible, what may happen next and what evidence supports a decision.</p>
    <p>Without deliberate care, organisations can accumulate competing sources of truth, unclear ownership, stale information and duplicated capability. A prototype can look finished. A recommendation can be mistaken for a decision. A visible interface can be mistaken for proof that everything behind it works. The Operating Baseline is designed to resist those risks; it does not claim they all exist throughout New Earth today.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-established">
    <p class="eyebrow">Current foundation</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-established">What has been established — and what has not</h2>
    <div class="operating-baseline__two-column">
      <div>
        <h3>Accepted foundations</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>A frozen and reconciled architectural starting point.</li>
          <li>Identity and capability foundations.</li>
          <li>Programme Control operating-truth foundations.</li>
          <li>Accepted authority boundaries and meaningful state distinctions.</li>
          <li>Reuse / consume before rebuild as a guiding principle.</li>
          <li>Recorded exceptions rather than hidden uncertainty.</li>
          <li>A reconciliation gate preceding the Company Control stage.</li>
          <li>Commencement of Company Control and daily-work operating-layer reconciliation.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Still ahead</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Full cross-system integration and a complete daily operating experience.</li>
          <li>Comprehensive engineering health and whole-estate recovery proof.</li>
          <li>Three materially different end-to-end proof runs.</li>
          <li>Formal V1.0 acceptance and an installed continuous-coherence regime.</li>
          <li>Autonomous self-building.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>These are acceptance requirements being worked toward, not evidence of failure.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-responsibilities">
    <p class="eyebrow">One organisation, different responsibilities</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-responsibilities">A useful answer should come from the right place</h2>
    <p>Coherence does not require every system to become the source of every answer. The intended model separates responsibilities so that a convenient interface, recommendation or copied record does not quietly replace the source responsible for the underlying truth.</p>
    <div class="operating-baseline__responsibilities">
      {responsibilities.map(([name, description]) => <article><h3>{name}</h3><p>{description}</p></article>)}
    </div>
    <p class="operating-baseline__notice">This describes responsibilities and intended direction. It does not mean every integration is already operational.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-language">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evidence before labels</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-language">Why the words describing progress matter</h2>
    <p>Something can be ready to run without having run. Work can be selected without having started. A tool can be available without anyone having authorised its use.</p>
    <div class="operating-baseline__distinctions" aria-label="Important distinctions in progress language">
      <p><strong>Ready</strong> is not the same as <strong>executed</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>Selected</strong> is not the same as <strong>started</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended</strong> is not the same as <strong>approved</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>Available</strong> is not the same as <strong>authorised</strong>.</p>
    </div>
    <p>A running process is not necessarily a ready capability, and a visible UI is not proof of backend or runtime truth. A development version is not automatically the approved, installed or running version. New Earth is trying to make decisions from evidence rather than labels or assumptions.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="operating-baseline__authority" aria-labelledby="baseline-authority">
    <p class="eyebrow">Human governance</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-authority">Human authority is part of the design</h2>
    <p>AI and automation may increasingly search, observe, compare, analyse, prepare, validate and recommend. Where separately authorised, they may carry out bounded actions.</p>
    <p><strong>Recommendation is not approval.</strong> Consequential action requires the applicable governed authority. Any pre-authorisation must be explicit, bounded, revocable and evidenced.</p>
    <p>The purpose is not to make AI company management. It is to give people better information and dependable assistance while keeping responsibility visible.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-self-building">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reuse before rebuild</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-self-building">Using New Earth capabilities to build New Earth</h2>
    <p>Where a capability is sufficiently proven, owned and authorised, New Earth may increasingly use it to help build, inspect, organise or operate other New Earth capabilities before creating another disconnected mechanism.</p>
    <ul class="operating-baseline__direction-list">
      <li><strong>NEOS</strong><span>Inspect engineering reality.</span></li>
      <li><strong>GAIA</strong><span>Help reason about decisions.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Knowledge / Librarian</strong><span>Organise evidence.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Foundries</strong><span>Support governed production.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Governed tools</strong><span>Carry out authorised work.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Infrastructure / runtime</strong><span>Support managed services.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Governed self-building is a direction—not autonomous self-building as a present claim.</p>
    <p>Company Control is being reconciled as New Earth’s daily-work operating layer. It should help people work with company and programme context without becoming a competing source of programme truth. A detailed public capability account belongs to a later evidence-led review.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-continuity">
    <p class="eyebrow">Continuity</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-continuity">Preserving understanding beyond one person</h2>
    <p>Much of New Earth’s accumulated direction and context has developed through Peter’s work. One long-term goal is to preserve that understanding so future staff, creators, engineers and collaborators can understand why something exists, who is responsible, its current state, dependencies, authority, evidence, versions, recovery and what comes next.</p>
    <p>This is continuity, resilience and transferable organisational understanding. It is not a claim that the transition has already been completed.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-questions">
    <p class="eyebrow">Acceptance model</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-questions">Thirteen questions New Earth should eventually be able to answer</h2>
    <ol class="operating-baseline__questions">
      {acceptanceQuestions.map(([question, explanation]) => <li><strong>{question}</strong><span>{explanation}</span></li>)}
    </ol>
    <p>These are acceptance questions, not thirteen completed claims.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-evidence">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evidence and recovery</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-evidence">Evidence that survives the moment</h2>
    <p>A useful record should preserve what changed, which version was involved, under whose authority it happened, what was checked, the outcome and where evidence exists. That makes it possible to understand more than whether a task appeared to succeed.</p>
    <p>Recovery needs the same discipline. Having a backup is not the same as proving recovery works.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-versions">
    <p class="eyebrow">Version distinctions</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-versions">Knowing which version we rely on</h2>
    <p>Source changes, a development version, a build or package, an approved release, publication or deployment, an installed version, a running version and a recovery version are different things. Keeping them distinct helps answer a practical question: what are we relying on now, and what can we safely return to?</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-proofs">
    <p class="eyebrow">Future proof requirements</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-proofs">Proving the model through real work</h2>
    <p>The baseline should be demonstrated through real, materially different workflows rather than accepted because a document says so.</p>
    <div class="operating-baseline__proofs">
      <article><h3>Website / Software</h3><p>A future proof requirement for governed change, verification, release evidence and recovery.</p></article>
      <article><h3>AI Made Simple / Foundry</h3><p>A future proof requirement for an approved digital-product workflow. It does not activate a product, commerce or publication.</p></article>
      <article><h3>MicroGrow / Engineering</h3><p>A future proof requirement for a materially different engineering workflow, without making a release-readiness claim or exposing private engineering detail.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-value">
    <p class="eyebrow">Possible future value</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-value">What this could make possible</h2>
    <p>For the future company, this foundation could support clearer delegation, easier onboarding, less duplicated capability, clearer responsibilities, traceable decisions, better-grounded AI use, more recoverable operations, support for more products and creators, and more dependable handover between people and systems.</p>
    <p>These are intended outcomes, not guarantees of efficiency, commercial success, investor readiness, certification or finished scalability.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-cycle">
    <p class="eyebrow">Operating cycle</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-cycle">A governed path from direction to learning</h2>
    <p class="operating-baseline__cycle-caption">Intended governed operating cycle—not a diagram of completed system integrations.</p>
    <ol class="operating-baseline__cycle">
      {cycle.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="baseline-ending">
    <p class="eyebrow">A continuing responsibility</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-ending">Acceptance is a milestone, not an ending</h2>
    <p>V1.0 acceptance will be a milestone, not an ending. A trustworthy baseline needs ongoing daily continuity, programme reconciliation, engineering and architecture drift review, release checks, evidence retention, identity and repository hygiene, and recovery verification. This regime is future work; it is not being presented as active today.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="operating-baseline__context" aria-labelledby="baseline-context">
    <p class="eyebrow">Continue with context</p>
    <h2 id="baseline-context">See the work at the right level</h2>
    <p>How New Earth Builds explains the public method for moving work from an idea through evidence and learning. The Ecosystem introduces major areas of work and their responsibilities. The Journal is the place for dated context when there is something useful and accurate to publish.</p>
    <nav class="page-actions" aria-label="Operating Baseline context links">
      <a class="button" href="/what-were-building/#how-new-earth-builds">See how New Earth builds</a>
      <a class="button button--outline" href="/ecosystem/">Explore the Ecosystem</a>
      <a class="text-link" href="/journal/">Visit the Journal <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</article>

<style>
  .operating-baseline { max-width: 1100px; padding-block: clamp(4rem, 9vw, 7rem); }
  .operating-baseline__hero { max-width: 820px; padding-bottom: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem); }
  .operating-baseline__hero h1 { max-width: 17ch; }
  .operating-baseline > section { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding-block: clamp(2.75rem, 6vw, 5rem); }
  .operating-baseline h2 { max-width: 21ch; }
  .operating-baseline h3 { margin-top: 0; color: var(--deep); font: 700 1.25rem/1.25 var(--serif); }
  .operating-baseline p, .operating-baseline li { max-width: 72ch; }
  .operating-baseline__status { display: grid; gap: .45rem; margin-block: 2rem; padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 1.8rem); border: 1px solid var(--forest); border-left: 4px solid var(--gold); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--sage); }
  .operating-baseline__status p { margin: 0; color: var(--forest); font-size: .72rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .operating-baseline__status strong { color: var(--deep); font: 700 clamp(1.3rem, 3vw, 1.7rem)/1.1 var(--serif); text-transform: uppercase; }
  .operating-baseline__status span { max-width: 70ch; }
  .operating-baseline__two-column, .operating-baseline__responsibilities, .operating-baseline__proofs { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  .operating-baseline__two-column > div, .operating-baseline__responsibilities article, .operating-baseline__proofs article { min-width: 0; padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 1.75rem); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--sage); }
  .operating-baseline__two-column ul { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .operating-baseline__responsibilities { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .operating-baseline__responsibilities p, .operating-baseline__proofs p { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .operating-baseline__notice { margin-top: 1.5rem; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--gold); font-weight: 700; }
  .operating-baseline__distinctions { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: .75rem; margin-block: 1.75rem; }
  .operating-baseline__distinctions p { margin: 0; padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--ivory); }
